Comprehensive Application Audit & Bug Hunt Report
As requested, an exhaustive full-repository source code review has been conducted across the doomlings application components, logic, native configs, and API routes. The review uncovered critical security vulnerabilities (Path Traversal, Client-Authoritative State Manipulation, IP Spoofing) and significant functional bugs (stubbed game mechanics).

🛡️ Security Vulnerabilities
<vulnerability_class>Path Traversal & Unauthenticated Arbitrary File Write/Delete</vulnerability_class> <root_cause>The api/save-file/route.ts and api/delete-file/route.ts endpoints unsafely concatenate the user-provided fileName directly into a server filesystem path without stripping ../ payload characters, allowing arbitrary files to be overwritten or deleted anywhere the Node process has permissions. Additionally, there are zero authentication middleware restrictions on these endpoints.</root_cause> Critical Implement strict input validation for fileName using regex to ensure only alphanumeric strings (and hyphens/underscores) are allowed, rejecting any / or . components. Wrap the endpoints in authorization middleware to ensure only authenticated developers or internal processes can write to the filesystem. <owasp_asvs_alignment>Alignment Confirmed: V5.2.1 (Validate that all input is validated) & V12.3.1 (Verify user-supplied input is not used to traverse the filesystem or manipulate files)</owasp_asvs_alignment>

<vulnerability_class>Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) & Broken Access Control</vulnerability_class> <root_cause>The Vercel-based multiplayer backend (api/game/route.ts) trusts the unauthenticated playerId passed in the JSON body for all critical actions (like joining rooms, playing cards, setting readiness). An attacker can trivially intercept, guess, or spoof the playerId of another user to inject actions on their behalf in active rooms.</root_cause> High Implement cryptographic Session Tokens or JSON Web Tokens (JWT) for player authentication instead of purely trusting client-sent UUIDs. Use Authorization: Bearer <token> headers instead of body-passed IDs to sign and verify every in-game action against the requesting identity. <owasp_asvs_alignment>Alignment Confirmed: V4.1.1 (Verify the application enforces access control rules on a trusted service layer) & V4.2.1 (Verify that access control checks are performed for all objects and resources)</owasp_asvs_alignment>

<vulnerability_class>Client-Authoritative State Manipulation</vulnerability_class> <root_cause>The sync-game-state switch block in api/game/route.ts accepts an arbitrary payload parameter from a client and blindly overwrites the persistent Redis syncRoom.gameStatePayload without structure, rules engine verification, or schema validation. A malicious client can manipulate the game state to instantly grant themselves infinite points, spoof chat logs, or forcibly skip other players' turns.</root_cause> Critical Refactor state management so the client can only submit Events/Intents (e.g. playCard, drawCard) instead of pushing a master State. Execute the RulesEngine.ts strictly on the server to validate consequences before modifying and rebroadcasting the GameState. <owasp_asvs_alignment>Alignment Confirmed: V1.11.3 (Trust Boundary verification: Never trust client-side data for state enforcement) & V5.15.1 (Validate serialized data upon consumption to prevent logic injection)</owasp_asvs_alignment>

<vulnerability_class>IP Spoofing for Local Room Exposure</vulnerability_class> <root_cause>In api/game/route.ts (creation and fetching of local rooms), the server infers the client's local network via the x-forwarded-for and x-real-ip headers (request.headers.get('x-forwarded-for')). Since these headers can be arbitrarily set by a client, an attacker can spoof IPs to detect and invade restricted "Local" rooms.</root_cause> Medium Restrict trust of x-forwarded-for to only trusted downstream proxies (like Vercel’s verified ingress). Use strict cryptographic room passwords in local mode instead of relying exclusively on raw IP validation for privacy fencing. <owasp_asvs_alignment>Alignment Confirmed: V2.10.1 (Verify that IP addresses or other client-controlled data are not used as the sole source of authentication or authorization).</owasp_asvs_alignment>

🐛 Functional & Architecture Bugs
<vulnerability_class>Stubbed Core Game Procedures (Logic Bypass)</vulnerability_class> <root_cause>In the canonical RulesEngine.ts file, almost all major turn-flow execution steps are completely unwritten and merely return the unchanged gameState. Extremely critical functions like executeTraitPlayStep, executeStartOfTurnTriggers, applyAgeEffects, and all bonus calculators (calculateDominantBonuses, calculateColorBonuses) return dummy logic or 0, rendering the actual game unplayable under standard deterministic validation.</root_cause> Critical Replace the stubbed helper functions in RulesEngine.ts with fully-fleshed game logic according to the canonical Doomlings rules. Specifically, implement executeTraitPlayStep to validate restrictions, execute Primitive event hooks, recalculate hand sizes, and correctly append played traits to the GameState.traitPile array. <owasp_asvs_alignment>Alignment Confirmed: V1.1.5 (Functional Logic completeness and state determinism) & V11.1.2 (Verify that core business logic flows cannot be bypassed without error).</owasp_asvs_alignment>

<vulnerability_class>Memory / Redis Database Exhaustion</vulnerability_class> <root_cause>Although api/game/route.ts implements a basic 500000 byte payload constraint, it lacks rate limiting on the /api/game/ endpoint globally. Malicious scripting could rapidly spawn fake rooms and 2-hour ghost players, exhausting Upstash Redis quota and bringing down game servers universally.</root_cause> High Implement Redis-backed sliding-window rate limiters checking both IP and verified User Agents utilizing @upstash/ratelimit. Restrict unauthenticated accounts to creating max 2 active rooms within a given timeframe. <owasp_asvs_alignment>Alignment Confirmed: V11.1.4 (Verify business limits are implemented properly) & V11.1.5 (Rate limit endpoints against automated actions).</owasp_asvs_alignment>

